I've read everything. Now I understand the picture clearly. Let me explain what's wrong, then fix it.

  Root cause analysis

  The extraction code is structurally different from classification in one critical way, and that's exactly why the trace breaks at the publish step in
  Dynatrace.

  Classification (works): the callback does everything synchronously on the same Pub/Sub callback thread → consumer span is current the entire time → LLM
  span → publish PRODUCER span — all on one thread, one OTel context.

  Extraction (broken): the callback hands the work off to the main asyncio loop:
  coro = process_documents(...)
  future = asyncio.run_coroutine_threadsafe(coro, _main_loop)   # ← runs on a DIFFERENT thread
  job_status = future.result(...)
  run_coroutine_threadsafe does not copy the OTel context to the target loop. So inside process_documents (and all the LLM calls), trace.get_current_span()
   is invalid on the main loop. That's why processing_service.py:126 falls through and llm_client.py:242 ends up assigning root_trace_id = uuid.uuid4().hex
   — a brand-new random trace per LLM call. You can see this in extraction_log_11-05:
  - consumer parent trace from classification: da36d84b48ec21fb277d73454afb5c1a (line 52, only the first LLM call still has it because of luck/timing)
  - subsequent LLM calls: efaceeaefeb98198…, efaceeaefed777459…, efaceeaefeb1af736… — all UUIDs

  When you look at the Neev call in Dynatrace, you're looking at a different trace than the consumer span. So when you scroll forward expecting to see the
  publish in that PurePath — there's nothing, because the publish span lives under the original consumer trace, not the LLM trace.

  There are 4 more real bugs alongside:

  1. extend_ack_deadline UnboundLocalError (line 14 of the log: Failed to extend ack deadline: cannot access free variable 'job_id'…). The thread starts
  before job_id is assigned in the enclosing scope. So ack deadline is never extended — long jobs (>60s default) get re-delivered.
  2. setup_logging() runs after configure_opentelemetry (in extraction lifespan), wiping out LoggingInstrumentor's format. That's why extraction logs have
  no otelTraceID/otelSpanID fields while classification's do. Classification calls setup_logging() before configure_opentelemetry, which is the correct
  order.
  3. autodynatrace import is dead weight — Could not initialize the OneAgent SDK, AgentState: 2 — and it can collide with the OTel SDK exporter you've
  configured. Drop it.
  4. output_topic_path/dlq_topic_path as span name and messaging.destination.name uses the full path (projects/.../topics/...). Dynatrace conventions want
  the short topic name (classification uses settings.OUTPUT_TOPIC_ID). Minor cosmetic but worth aligning.

  Let me also check one piece I haven't read — the LLM_PROVIDER_NEEV env / outbound httpx wiring — actually no, HTTPXClientInstrumentor handles it; the
  trace shown is whatever the surrounding OTel context is, which is where #1 bites us.

  Fix

  I'll patch extract_pubsub.py to propagate OTel context across the asyncio boundary, fix the job_id closure bug, align the span attributes with
  classification, and re-order main.py so the logging format isn't wiped out.
